/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Take a p-type body with acceptor doping NA (cm⁻³), so holes are the majority carriers.
   - The flat-band voltage VFB is the gate-to-body voltage at which the surface holds no net charge.
   - The capacitance-voltage (C-V) curve shows the same states.
   - Add n⁺ source (S) and drain (D) regions on either side of the gate (G); with the body (B) the device has four terminals.
   - With VDS = 0 and VGS > VT, a uniform channel joins source and drain.
   - Now raise VDS.
   - Cutoff: no channel, only leakage (see Leakage and subthreshold conduction).
   - A pMOS is the mirror image: n-type body (an n-well in CMOS), p⁺ source and drain, a hole channel.
   - Some syllabi speak of characteristics "under static and dynamic conditions": static is the DC behaviour in these regions (I-V characteristics); dynamic is the charging of the device capacitances (capacitances and parasitic resistances).
   - In logic a transistor is a switch.
*/
