/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Take an nMOS on a p body with the source tied to the body.
   - VT0 = VFB + 2φF + √(2qεsiNA·2φF)/Cox
   - Here VT0 is the threshold with VSB = 0; εsi ≈ 1.04 × 10⁻¹⁰ F/m is the permittivity of silicon; Cox = εox/tox; NA is in m⁻³ when the other quantities are in SI units.
   - With an n⁺ polysilicon gate, φMS ≈ −(0.56 + φF) V for a p body, so VT0 often comes out close to zero.
   - Now let the source rise above the body by VSB = VS − VB > 0.
   - VT = VT0 + γ[√(2φF + VSB) − √(2φF)], with γ = √(2qεsiNA)/Cox
   - γ is the body-effect coefficient in √V (typically 0.2 to 0.5 √V); heavier doping or a thicker oxide makes it larger.
   - In a two-input NAND, the lower nMOS has its source at ground, but the upper nMOS has its source at the node between them.
   - For a pMOS in an n-well the same terms appear with opposite signs: VTp is negative, the body effect makes |VTp| larger when the well sits above the source (VBS > 0), and the formula uses |φF|, the donor doping ND and VBS in place of VSB.
   - Temperature lowers |VT| by roughly 1 to 2 mV per kelvin, because φF shrinks as ni grows.
*/
