/* book Worked example: Q: An nMOS has NA = 10¹⁷ cm⁻³, tox = 4 nm, an n⁺ polysilicon gate and fixed oxide charge Qox/q = 2 × 10¹⁰ cm⁻². Use εox = 3.45 × 10⁻¹¹ F/m, εsi = 1.04 × 10⁻¹⁰ F/m, ni = 1.5 × 10¹⁰ cm⁻³, kT/q = 0.026 V and φMS = −(0.56 + φF) V. (a) Find VT0. (b) What threshold-adjust dose raises it to 0.45 V?
   1. Units: NA = 10¹⁷ cm⁻³ = 10²³ m⁻³; Qox = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 2 × 10¹⁴ m⁻² = 3.2 × 10⁻⁵ C/m².
   2. Cox = 3.45 × 10⁻¹¹ / 4 × 10⁻⁹ = 8.625 × 10⁻³ F/m².
   3. φF = 0.026 × ln(10¹⁷ / 1.5 × 10¹⁰) = 0.026 × 15.713 = 0.4085 V, so 2φF = 0.8171 V.
   4. φMS = −(0.56 + 0.4085) = −0.9685 V; Qox/Cox = 3.2 × 10⁻⁵ / 8.625 × 10⁻³ = 0.0037 V; VFB = −0.9685 − 0.0037 = −0.9722 V.
   5. Qdep = √(2 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 1.04 × 10⁻¹⁰ × 10²³ × 0.8171) = 1.649 × 10⁻³ C/m²; Qdep/Cox = 0.1912 V.
   6. VT0 = −0.9722 + 0.8171 + 0.1912 = 0.036 V.
   7. Dose: ΔVT = 0.45 − 0.036 = 0.414 V, so DI = ΔVT·Cox/q = 0.414 × 8.625 × 10⁻³ / 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 2.23 × 10¹⁶ m⁻² = 2.23 × 10¹² cm⁻².
   Answer: VT0 ≈ 0.036 V, almost zero; a boron dose of about 2.2 × 10¹² cm⁻² raises it to 0.45 V. */
