/* book Worked example 2: Q: The adjusted device of the first example (VT0 = 0.45 V, NA = 10²³ m⁻³, Cox = 8.625 × 10⁻³ F/m², 2φF = 0.8171 V) is the upper transistor of a stack, with its source 1 V above the body. Find γ, the new VT, and the fall in saturation current at VGS = 1.2 V using ID ∝ (VGS − VT)².
   1. γ = √(2 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 1.04 × 10⁻¹⁰ × 10²³) / 8.625 × 10⁻³ = 1.824 × 10⁻³ / 8.625 × 10⁻³ = 0.2115 √V.
   2. ΔVT = 0.2115 × (√1.8171 − √0.8171) = 0.2115 × (1.3480 − 0.9039) = 0.0939 V.
   3. VT = 0.45 + 0.0939 = 0.544 V.
   4. Current ratio = (1.2 − 0.544)² / (1.2 − 0.45)² = 0.656² / 0.75² = 0.430 / 0.5625 = 0.765.
   Answer: γ = 0.21 √V; VT rises from 0.45 V to 0.544 V; the saturation current falls by about 23 %. */
